sections:
  - eyebrow: "The Problem With Good Intentions"
    heading: "Intentions Don't Show Up In An Audit"
    body: "It is easy for any organization to say it cares about safety, fairness, and transparency. It is much harder to point to the specific mechanisms that make those commitments real: who reviews a model before it ships, what happens when it behaves unexpectedly, and what a user is told about its limitations. Without those mechanisms, responsible AI is a sentence in a footer, not a practice."
  - eyebrow: "Our Perspective"
    heading: "Human Oversight Has To Be Designed In, Not Assumed"
    body: "At VARLENYX, we treat AI as a tool that supports human judgment rather than a system that operates independently of it. That distinction only means something if it is reflected in how systems are built: clear boundaries on what a system is intended to do, explicit points where a person reviews or can override an output, and honesty about where a capability is still experimental."
